Let's double-check the logic of placing bulb at i+1. When i > cur, we place at i+1. Why i+1? Because a bulb at i+1 covers [i, i+2] (since (i+1)-1 = i, (i+1)+1 = i+2). This ensures position i is covered, and we extend coverage to i+2, which is the maximum rightward extension for a single bulb starting at or after i. If we placed at i, it would cover [i-1, i+1], extending only to i+1, which is worse. If i+1 >= n, we place at i (which is n-1), covering [n-2, n-1]. That's the best we can do at the end.

One edge case: what if i+1 < n but placing at i+1 might overlap with a working bulb that we haven't processed yet? The algorithm processes i sequentially, and cur already incorporates working bulbs with left <= i. When we place an additional bulb, we set cur = min(n-1, place+1). This might be less than some future working bulb's reach, but the loop will continue and when i advances, cur will be updated with max_reach[i] which includes those working bulbs. So it's fine.

But wait: in the loop, after placing a bulb, we do `cur = min(n-1, place + 1)`. Then we increment i. Next iteration, we do `cur = max(cur, max_reach[i])`. This correctly takes the max of the additional bulb's reach and any working bulb reach at position i. However, what if the additional bulb's reach extends beyond i, and we skip some i's? The loop increments i by 1 each time, so we check every i. That's O(n) which is fine for n=1e5.

But is it possible that after placing a bulb at i+1, cur becomes i+2 (capped), and then for the next i (which is i+1), we have i+1 <= cur, so we don't place another bulb. That's correct because the bulb at i+1 covers i+1 as well.

