Now, let's double-check the logic of `new_cur = place + 1` capped at `n-1`. In the code I wrote:
```python
new_cur = place + 1
if new_cur > n - 1:
    new_cur = n - 1
```
This is equivalent to `new_cur = min(n-1, place+1)`. That's correct because a bulb at `place` covers up to `place+1` (inclusive), but if `place+1 >= n`, the last position is `n-1`.

But wait: in the algorithm description earlier, I had `covered_until = min(n-1, place + 1)`. And then I used `cur = new_cur`. But in the loop, `cur` is the rightmost covered position (inclusive). When we check `if i > cur`, `i` is the current position index. If `cur` is inclusive, then positions `0..cur` are covered. So if `cur = n-1`, all positions covered. That matches.

But in the step where we update `cur` with working bulbs: `if max_reach[i] > cur: cur = max_reach[i]`. Here `max_reach[i]` is the farthest right position reachable by a working bulb with left endpoint i. But is `max_reach[i]` inclusive? Yes, we set `right = min(n-1, i+v)`, which is inclusive. So `cur` is inclusive. Good.

Now, one subtle point: when we place an additional bulb at `place = i+1`, we set `cur = min(n-1, place+1)`. But what if there was already a working bulb reach that extends further right, and we overwrite `cur` with a smaller value? We do `if i > cur: ... cur = new_cur`. But what if `cur` was already larger than `new_cur`? The condition `if i > cur` would be false if `cur` is already >= i. So we only enter the block if `i > cur`. At that point, `cur` is < i. Since `new_cur = place+1 = i+2` (if place=i+1), and i > cur, we have i >= cur+1, so i+2 >= cur+3 > cur. So `new_cur` will always be > cur. So we are increasing `cur`. Good.

But what if `place = i` (because i+1 >= n)? Then `new_cur = i+1`. Since i > cur, and i is n-1 (the last index), `cur` was < n-1, so `new_cur = n-1` (capped) which is > cur. Good.

Now, is there any case where after placing a bulb, we should also consider working bulbs that start at positions we've already passed? The loop goes forward, and we only update `cur` with `max_reach[i]` at the current `i`. What if a working bulb has left endpoint < i, but we haven't processed its `max_reach` because we skipped it? No, we process every i from 0 to n-1. For each i, we do `cur = max(cur, max_reach[i])`. So if a working bulb has left endpoint j < i, we would have processed `max_reach[j]` when i was j. And since `cur` is the running maximum, it will carry forward the maximum right reach from all working bulbs with left <= i. So by the time we reach i, `cur` already includes the effect of all working bulbs starting at or before i. This is the key insight from the taps problem.

